# Engineering the Activity and Stability of MOF‐Nanocomposites for Efficient Water Oxidation.

## abstract
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered to be promising candidates for electrochemical water splitting. However, most MOFs are characterized by low electronic conductivity limiting their use as bulk materials for anodes and cathodes. Furthermore, the understanding of the critical parameters controlling the activity and stability of MOF electrocatalysts is still insufficient. Herein, a systematic analysis is presented of the key structural parameters controlling the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performance and stability of a representative family of bimetallic NiFe‐MOFs, where the role of the metal cations on the accessible active sites and intrinsic activity can be investigated independently from the crystal structure. The models and in‐depth structural and morphological characterizations reveal a hierarchy of properties affecting the OER activity with accessible sites and intrinsic activity playing a major role in the charge transfer efficiency. Optimization of these properties and addition of a conductive support substrate leads to efficient MOF‐nanocomposite electrocatalysts achieving a low overpotential of 258 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm−2 with a small Tafel slope of 49 mV dec−1 and excellent stability for more than 32 h of continuous OER in alkaline medium.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered to be promising candidates for electrochemical water splitting. However, most MOFs are characterized by low electronic conductivity limiting their use as bulk materials for anodes and cathodes. Furthermore, the understanding of the critical parameters controlling the activity and stability of MOF electrocatalysts is still insufficient. Herein, a systematic analysis is presented of the key structural parameters controlling the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performance and stability of a representative family of bimetallic NiFeMOFs, where the role of the metal cations on the accessible active sites and intrinsic activity can be investigated independently from the crystal structure. The models and in-depth structural and morphological characterizations reveal a hierarchy of properties affecting the OER activity with accessible sites and intrinsic activity playing a major role in the charge transfer efficiency. Optimization of these properties and addition of a conductive support substrate leads to efficient MOF-nanocomposite electrocatalysts achieving a low overpotential of $258~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{v}$ at a current density of $\mathsf{10\ m A\ c m^{-2}}$ with a small Tafel slope of $49\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ and excellent stability for more than $32\ h$ of continuous OER in alkaline medium.
## 1. Introduction
Water electrolysis is widely regarded as one of the most promising technologies for production of green hydrogen with renewable energy. However, the required oxygen evolution reaction (OER), is thermodynamically unfavorable and usually demands large overpotentials to reach appreciable catalytic currents.[1] At present, precious metal catalysts (such as Ru and Ir) have demonstrated excellent OER activity and stability in acidic and basic media. However, the scarcity of these precious metals is a restricting factor for large-scale production.[2] The development of OER catalysts using earthabundant metals such as Co, Ni, Fe, Mn is the focus of significant efforts.[3] In particular, the synergistic modulation of activity and stability through metal cation doping and their roles on the electrocatalytic performance has been focused of some recent studies.[4] Amongst others, metal– organic frameworks (MOFs) have received increasing attention for electrocatalysis.[5] Owing to the versatility of their composition and crystal structure, MOFs can provide abundant catalytically active sites accessible to the reactants.[6] Nevertheless, the majority of MOFs suffer of poor electronic conductivity limiting the overall conversion efficiency of electrical in chemical energy,[5a] and the MOF organic linkers have usually mediocre chemical stability, resulting in poor long-term performance.[7]
A promising approach to overcome these challenges is to engineering very thin MOF catalysts, such as $2\mathsf{D}^{[7,8]}$ . For example, ultrathin Ni–Co MOF nanosheets with coordinatively unsaturated metal sites exhibited significantly enhanced OER activity compared to bulk Ni–Co MOFs.[5c] Duan et al.[5b] developed an ultrathin nickel–iron based MOF array on Ni foam via a facile one-step chemical bath deposition method, which delivered a promising electrocatalytic performance for both the OER and the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), achieving a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at a cell voltage of $1.55~\mathrm{V}.$ Huang et  al.[9] applied an electrochemical/chemical exfoliation method to synthesize 2D Co-MOFs, demonstrating their use as efficient electrocatalysts for OER. However, despite the large active surface area of 2D MOFs, they still face the issues of intrinsically low conductivity when clustered in larger scale electrodes.[10] An alternative is to engineer composite structures of thin MOF grains on electrically conductive porous supports. MOF/graphene composites have been explored for applications in Li–S batteries[11] and supercapacitors[12]. Despite these achievements, current understanding of the critical structural parameters controlling the activity and stability of MOF electrocatalysts is insufficient, resulting in mixed performances.[13]
Herein, we present a systematic analysis of the key structural parameters controlling the OER performance and stability of a representative family of bimetallic NiFe-MOF. The latter composition was chosen due to the feasibility of forming well-controlled Ni–Fe solid solutions with a NiBTC-MOF crystal structure, enabling to study the role of specific metal cation sites on the accessibility of active sites and intrinsic electrochemical activity. We develop a facile strategy for the preparation of small NiFe-MOF grains uniformly distributed on reduced graphene oxide nanosheets (NiFe-MOF/G) with tunable Ni/Fe ratios. The resulting NiFeMOF/G composite structure features both a high concentration of accessible active metal sites and enhanced charge transfer efficiency. Our combined modelling and experiments reveal that the substitutional doping of Fe cations in Ni sites significantly increases the accessible active sites and decreases the free energies for the rate-determined OER step, resulting in a high intrinsic activity. Optimal NiFe-MOF/G nanocomposites achieve a low overpotential of $258~\mathrm{mV}$ for $10\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ and excellent stability for more than $30\mathrm{~h~}$ continuous OER in alkaline conditions. We believe that these insights on the role of metal centers on the OER performance of MOFs and this flexible preparation strategy provide promising directions for the engineering of future MOFbased electrocatalysts.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterization of NiFe-MOF/G
MOF/reduced graphene oxide nanocomposites were synthesized through a two-step thermal solvent strategy, schematically shown in Figure 1a. First, MOF precursors with a broad range of Ni–Fe molar ratios (0:1, 2:1, 3:1, 4:1, 5:1, and 1:0) were grafted on graphene oxide (GO, Hummers’ method) by self-assembly of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/F e^{3+}}$ with GO nanosheets in an ethylene glycol solvent at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Here, the positively charged $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/F e^{3+}}$ cations homogeneously adsorbed on the negatively charged GO surfaces via electrostatic interaction. Simultaneously, the GO was reduced, hereinafter referred to as graphene (G), by reaction with the ethylene glycol, significantly improving the conductivity of the catalyst. The resulting MOF precursors/G were ultrasonically dispersed in ethanol and mixed with benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (BTC) under stirring at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form monometallic and bimetallic M-MOF/G (M $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Fe, Ni, NiFe). These result in the formation of very small $(<10\ \mathrm{nm})$ MOF grains in intimate contact with the graphene lamella (Figure  1b–e). Unless otherwise specified, only samples with a 4:1 molar ratio of Ni–Fe (named NiFe-MOF/G) is characterized and discussed in the following, which is due to its superior OER activity compared to other samples. The details of the synthesis method are reported in the Supporting Information.
The formation of ultrafine MOF grains is confirmed by high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) analysis (Figure  1b–d; Figures  S1 and S2, Supporting Information). It is notable that the NiFe-MOF is confined in small nanodots (size $<10~\mathrm{nm}$ ) uniformly dispersed on the graphene nanosheets. The homogenous dispersion of the MOF grains originates from the uniform dispersion of the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/F e^{3+}}$ precursors on graphene nanosheets (Figure S3, Supporting Information). For comparison, Ni-MOF/G (Figure S4, Supporting Information) and Fe-MOF/G (Figure S5, Supporting Information) were also prepared, resulting in a similar morphology to the NiFe-MOF, except that the Fe-MOF/G exhibits a larger MOF crystal size and more clustering on the graphene. HRTEM shows that the crystalline NiFe-MOF domains have a lattice spacing of $0.22~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  1e). This spacing corresponds to the Ni-MOF (220) plane $(0.22\ \mathrm{nm}$ , Figure S4, Supporting Information) and is distinct from the spacing observed in the Fe-MOF crystals $(0.25~\mathrm{nm}$ , Figure S5, Supporting Information).[14] These results are also consistent with the X-ray diffraction (XRD) Ni-MOF/G patterns (Figure S6, Supporting Information) that are in line with those previously reported for the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ MOF.[14a] The sharp peak at $17.4^{\circ}$ and $18.7^{\circ}$ refers to the crystalline facet of (220) and (111), indicating a good crystallinity of the Ni-MOF/G.[14] The Fe-MOF/G has lower crystallinity and a distinct crystal structure than the Ni-MOF/G, in line with the literature.[15] Importantly, the NiFe-MOF/G has also the same crystal structure as the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ MOF, suggesting the formation of a solid solution with substitutional or interstitial doping of Fe in a Ni-determined crystal structure.
The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) further confirms the composition of these MOF/G nanocomposites (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Two stages of weight loss in the TGA curves of the Ni-MOF/G and NiFe-MOF/G are assigned to the loss of crystallization water molecules (from 100 to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and the decomposition of the organic frameworks (from 250 to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , respectively. The final products are NiO, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and graphene.[14b] It is noted that Fe-MOF/G has only one stage weight loss of organic frameworks, suggesting no crystallization water in Fe-MOF structure. This is in line with the nitrogen adsorption isotherms (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The specific surface area (SSA) of Ni-MOF/G, Fe$\mathrm{MOF/G}$ , and NiFe-MOF/G were determined to be 89, 924, and
$437{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. The TGA analysis confirms that some crystal water may remains trapped in the Ni-MOF for temperature significantly above $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S7, Supporting Information), suggesting that not all the surface area may be accessible during the BET analysis, despite the prior degassing for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The precise composition of the NiFe-MOF/G sample was determined by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES), indicating $75\pm1.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ NiFe-MOF content with a Ni:Fe molar ratio of 3.9:1 with Ni and Fe 13.0 and $3.22~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively.
X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy were used to study the coordination structure and chemical state of the NiFe-MOF/G. The Fe K-edge XANES spectra of the NiFe$\mathrm{MOF/G}$ (Figure 1f) shows a significantly higher energy absorption edge than that of Fe foil and FeO, while it nearly overlaps with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , suggesting a $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ valence state. The Ni K-edge XANES spectra of the NiFe-MOF/G (Figure  1g) shows an absorption edge position between that of Ni foil and a NiO standard, indicating a valence state between ${\mathrm{Ni}^{0}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ .[16] The EXAFS spectra of the NiFe-MOF/G in the R space without phase correction (Figure  1h,i) provide further information on its coordination. The peak at $\mathrm{~\i.54~\AA}$ refers to Fe–O coordination (Figure 1h) confirming an oxidized state and agreeing well with that reported for iron oxide.[5d] Compared to the FeO and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ , the weaker intensity and longer radial distance of the peak at $2.80\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is ascribed to Fe–Fe shell,[5d] indicate a smaller first coordination number and a larger bond length than iron oxides. Furthermore, the two peaks at 1.58 and $2.7\bar{5}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ are assigned to the nearest shell coordination of Ni–O and Ni– Ni, respectively.[5d] It is also noted that the NiFe-MOF/G has a larger Ni–Ni bond length than NiO (Figure 1i).
## 2.2. Electrocatalytic Performance
To assess the electrocatalytic OER performance of these NiFe$\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{G}$ , polarization curves of the samples were collected in a three-electrode system in $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ electrolyte at a scan rate of $5~\mathrm{\mV~\s^{-1}}$ (Figure  2a; Figure S9, Supporting Information). The Faraday efficiency (FE) for OER was calculated by using a rotating ring disk electrode method (RRDE) and obtained a
FE of $98.6\pm2.8\%$ for the NiFe-MOF/G (Figure S10, Supporting Information). This confirms that the catalytic current was entirely attributed to the oxygen evolution with no side reactions occurring.
The monometallic Ni-MOF/G and $_{\mathrm{Fe-MOF/G}}$ , used as a comparison, show moderate OER activities with onset overpotentials of 363 and $404~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively. In contrast, the NiFe-MOF/G exhibits elevated activity, showing low overpotentials of only 258 and $326~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of 10 and $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively. Notably, these NiFe$\mathsf{M O F/G}$ are also superior to the $\mathrm{Ir/C}$ benchmark catalyst that displays an overpotential of $340~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In comparison, bulk NiFe-MOF shows significantly less activity for OER with an overpotential of $332\mathrm{mV}$ for $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . This is attributed to the NiFe-MOF/G nanocomposite morphology having well-dispersed ultrafine MOF grains with a large number of exposed active sites and reduced electrical losses. With respect to the Tafel plots (Figure  2b), NiFe-MOF/G also exhibits the smallest linear slope of $49~\mathrm{\mV~dec^{-1}}$ , indicating the most favorable reaction kinetics for the OER. This slope is similar to that of bulk NiFe-MOF $(50\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1})}$ but much smaller than that of monometallic Fe-MOF/G $(77\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1})}$ and Ni-MOF/G $(79~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ , suggesting that the formation of a bimetallic NiFe-MOF structure plays an important role in the intrinsic OER activity of the nanocomposites.
In order to quantify the intrinsic OER activity of this MOF/G nanocomposite, the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of all the electrocatalysts was calculated through the double-layer capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ method (Figures S11 and S12, Supporting Information). The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value of the NiFe-MOF/G $(\approx15\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) is more than twice higher than that of the other catalysts $(<7.0\ \mathrm{mF\cm^{-2}})$ ), indicating an increased ECSA. The higher intrinsic OER activity of the NiFe-MOF/G system was confirmed by normalization with the corresponding ECSA (Figure 2c). Comparison of the TOF of these catalyst systems at a potential of $1.58{\mathrm{V}}{}$ versus RHE further confirms the significantly higher activity of the NiFe-MOF/G with $1.80~\mathrm{~s~}^{-1}$ , followed by NiFe-MOF, Ni-MOF/G, Fe-MOF/G with a TOF of 0.25, 0.07, and $0.02{\mathrm{~s}}^{-1} $ , respectively.
The charge transfer kinetics of these well-dispersed and ultrafine NiFe-MOF grains on graphene was investigated by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). Figure S13 (Supporting Information) shows the Nyquist plots of the samples with the suggested equivalent circuit and fitting results in the inset. The NiFe-MOF/G have the smallest charge transfer resistance $\ R_{\mathrm{ct}}=2.2$ ohm), implying efficient electron and mass transfer rates. The NiFe-MOF/G also exceeds the bulk NiFe-MOF. This is attributed to the good dispersion and small size of the MOF grains as well as their intimate contact with graphene. Importantly, the chronopotentiometric results show that NiFe-MOF/G has excellent stability, with a stable potential for more than $32\mathrm{~h~}$ at a constant current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ in $1\mathrm{~m~}$ KOH (Figure  2d). The stability of specific MOF catalytic systems for OER and HER varies significantly as a function of the organic ligands and their interaction with the metal cations. For instance, recent investigation of the MOF-74 system for OER revealed (oxy) hydroxides as active sites,[17] while other MOF systems such as CoBDC–Fc–NF,[18] $_{\mathrm{A}_{2.7}\mathrm{B}\cdot\mathrm{MOF}\cdot\mathrm{FeCo}_{1.6},}[1$ 9] and FeMOFs- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}[20]$ have been found to preserve their original structure during and/or post OER. To investigate the stability of our NiFe-MOF/G catalyst surface we characterized the post-OER composition of these sample by XPS, HRTEM, and Raman immediately after $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ OER reaction (Figure S14, Supporting Information). The XPS analysis of the NiFe-MOF/G after $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ OER (Figure S14 a,b, Supporting Information) show that the surface composition is preserved showing the same chemical states as the samples prior to OER (Figure 3e,f). The HRTEM analysis of the post-OER NiFe-MOF/G samples (Figure S14 c,d, Supporting Information) shows no significant agglomeration and restructuring of the MOF crystals after OER. Raman analysis of the NiFe-MOF/G samples before and after OER (Figure  S14e, Supporting Information) shows only two peaks, attributable to graphene, indicating insufficient sensitivity to the NiFe-MOF presence to verify its stability. Overall, these analysis show no obvious of agglomeration and phase transition of NiFe-MOF crystal to $\mathrm{{\bfM}}(\mathrm{{OH}})_{2}$ or MOOH suggesting that the chemical states and composition of this catalyst system remains stable during OER. The stability of the NiFe-MOF during OER was further tested as a comparison (Figure  S15, Supporting Information). It was observed that the OER activity of the pure NiFe-MOF decreases significantly after $10\mathrm{~h~}$ of continuous OER, further confirming the importance of the graphene support in stabilizing the MOF structure in NiFeMOF/G system. The activity and stability of these NiFe-MOF/G nanocomposites are comparable to the best reported for MOFbased electrocatalysts in alkaline conditions, while its stability exceeds most previous studies with the exception of NiCoUMOFNs[21] (Figure 2e; Table S1, Supporting Information).
## 2.3. Intrinsic OER Activity and Fe Cation Role
To understand the role of the Fe cations on the intrinsic activity of these NiFe-MOF/G nanocomposites, a series of characterizations and simulations were conducted. Energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images (illustrated by temperature colors) of NiFe-MOF/G (Figure 3a–d) shows that the material is composed of C, Fe, and Ni, in which Ni and Fe are uniformly distributed across the graphene nanosheet, further confirming the formation of NiFe-MOF solid solution with no obvious phase segregation.
The surface composition of the NiFe-MOF/G, Ni-MOF/G, and Fe-MOF/G were studied by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) as shown in Figure  3e-f. The Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ can be deconvoluted into four peaks at 855.9, 857.5, 861.8, and $865.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ which are ascribed to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ , and satellite peak of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ .[22] Similarly, the Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ can be deconvoluted into three peaks at 711.3, 714.6, and $719.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ which are assigned to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and satellite peak of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ .[23] The small shoulder peak at $707.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ of NiFe-MOF/G refers to Ni auger. For a better comparison, we have summarized the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}/N i^{2+}}$ ratios in Figure  3g. The bimetallic NiFe-MOF/G has a slight increase in the $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}/N i^{2+}}$ ratio compared to monometallic ${\mathrm{Ni-MOF/G}}$ , suggesting an electronic modification of Ni site by the presence of Fe in the Ni-MOF crystal structure. More notably, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ratio of the NiFe-MOF/G is more than twice that of the Fe-MOF/G, revealing that the Fe cations in the NiFe-MOF/G have a distinct electronic structures from the Fe in the Fe-MOF/G. The difference in the Ni valence state results by XANES $(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ) and XPS $(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}})$ is attributed the sensitivity of analysis method to the different part of the sample. XANES is more sensitive to the bulk composition of the sample, while XPS is very sensitive to its surface composition. As a result, the difference in the valance state indicated by XANES and XPS suggest the Ni sites in NiFe-MOF/G sample are more oxidized on the surface. The EIS analysis was further applied to probe the kinetics of the electrocatalytic reactions. The Bode plots were conducted (Figure 3h,i) revealing that the phase angle of the NiFe-MOF/G is much smaller than that of the NiMOF/G at each potential (Figure 3j). This indicates that for NiFe$\mathrm{MOF/G}$ , more electrons participate in the OER catalysis rather further supporting a higher density of active sites[24] as indicated by the higher ECSA.
To understand the higher intrinsic OER activity of our NiFeMOF/G nanocomposites, density functional theory (DFT)
calculations have been performed to determine the free energy of each intermediate during the OER process. Two reasonable models for NiFe-MOF/G and Ni-MOF/G were built according to their XRD patterns (Figure S16; Figure  4a,b, Supporting Information) and in line with those reported for $\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ $12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(M=Co}$ , Ni, and Zn).[14a] The model of NiFe-MOF/G consists of six BTC molecules, four Ni atoms and one Fe atom, which corresponds to a Ni:Fe molar ratio of 4:1 of the prepared NiFe-MOF/G sample. Figure 4c–e shows the free energy change diagrams of four reaction steps on the Ni active site of Ni-MOF/G, and on both the Ni and Fe active sites of the NiFe$\mathrm{MOF/G}$ . The free energy barrier (ΔG) of each intermediate adsorption on the host Ni site of the NiFe-MOF/G is very close to those on the original Ni site of the Ni-MOF/G (Figure 4c,d).
Overall, the above characterizations and modelling reveal that the OER activity of this representative family of bimetallic MOF is controlled by a not trivial interplay of ECSA, charge transfer efficiency and intrinsic activity. Notably, despite having the same Ni-BTC-MOF crystal structure, the substitutive doping of Fe cations on Ni sites drastically increase the accessible MOF surface area from 89 to $437{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m}}}^{2}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~g}}}^{-1}$ . This is mirrored by an increase of the ECSA from $5.4\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of the pure Ni-MOF to $15~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ of the NiFe-MOF (Figure S11, Supporting Information). This is attributed to the modelled decrease of the hydroxyl group binding energy arising from the Fe-doping (Figure S17, Supporting Information), which facilitates the desorption of water in line with the TGA analysis (Figure S7, Supporting Information). This also shifts the rate limiting reaction steps resulting in an increase of the intrinsic OER activity for the NiFe-MOF (Figure  4). Finally, the charge transfer resistance becomes a critical parameter in determining the overall OER efficiency, with the bulk NiFe-MOF requiring $80~\mathrm{mV}$ more overpotential than the NiFe-MOF/G nanocomposite to reach an OER current density of $200\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ .
## 3. Conclusion
We reported a systematic analysis of the critical parameters affecting the OER performance of a tunable family of MOFs. We also introduce a versatile strategy for the engineering of efficient and stable MOF-graphene nanocomposite electrocatalysts for water oxidation. Our NiFe-MOF/G nanocomposites feature a uniform dispersion of ultrafine grain MOF grains in intimate contact with the graphene that provides both a high density of accessible active sites and efficient charge transfer. Optimization of the NiFe-MOF/G structure results in an excellent OER performance with significantly lower overpotential and Tafel slope for OER than Ir benchmark catalysts, bulk NiFeMOF, and monometallic Fe- and Ni-MOF/G catalysts, and also better stability than most recent MOF OER catalysts reported. Our characterization and simulations reveal that this superior performance of the NiFe-MOF/G arises from the higher electrochemical active area, lower charge-transfer resistance and higher intrinsic activity, due to the substitutional doping of Fe cations on Ni sites of the Ni-BTC-MOF crystal structure. The latter reduces the free energy of adsorbed intermediate in the ratedetermining step further enhancing OER activity. We believe that these insights on the facile preparation of stable and efficient nanocomposite OER electrocatalysts provide a promising approach for the engineering of a broad family of MOF-based materials with numerous applications in electrochemistry.
